===Etymology===
[[File:Statue of Harry Hotspur in Alnwick.jpg|thumb|A statue of Sir [[w:Henry Percy (Hotspur)|Henry Percy]] ''(1364–1403; {{senseno|en|surname}})'' in [[Alnwick]], [[Northumberland]], [[England]], [[United Kingdom]]. The son of {{w|Henry Percy, 1st Earl of Northumberland}}, Percy was an English [[knight#Noun|knight]] who fought in several campaigns against the [[Scot#Noun|Scots]] in the northern border and against the [[French#Noun|French]] during the [[Hundred Years' War|Hundred Years’ War]]. He was nicknamed “Hotspur” by the Scots as a tribute to his speed in advance and readiness to attack.]]

The {{lg|proper noun}} {{senseno|en|surname}} (surname) and {{senseno|en|place name}} (place names) are derived from {{der|en|nrf|Percy|t=a baronial surname}}, from {{m|nrf|Percy-en-Auge|t=a place in Pays d’Auge, Normandy, France|lit=Percy in Auge}}, from {{der|en|fro|Perci}}, from {{der|en|LL.|Persiacum}}, from {{der|en|la|Persius|t=a {{w|Gallo-Roman}} [[nomen gentile]]}}, from {{der|en|ett|𐌐𐌄𐌓𐌔𐌖}}; further etymology uncertain, possibly from {{m|ett|𐌘𐌄𐌓𐌔𐌖|t=mask}}.

Proper noun {{senseno|en|given name}} (given name) is either from the above, or is a {{clipping|en|Percival|alt=Perc(ival)|nocap=1}} + {{affix|en|-y|pos1={{lg|suffix}} forming familiar names|id1=familiar}}.<ref>{{R:Collins English Dictionary|pos=n}}</ref>

The common {{lg|noun}} is derived from the proper noun.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=1036768500|date=December 2023}}</ref> {{senseno|en|penis|uc=1}} (“penis”) is probably from the fact that ''Percy'' and {{m|en|penis}} begin with the same letter.

